# SCOPIO backend -- which physical instrument is which.
#
#   cp .env.example .env        # .env is gitignored: it describes THIS rig
#   docker compose up -d        # compose reads .env automatically
#
# This is the one place the Pi's hardware wiring is written down. Nothing here
# is a secret (API keys live in secrets/api_keys.json); it is per-machine, which
# is why it is not in git.
#
# Change a value -> `docker compose up -d` again (recreates the containers).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- Instrument addresses (VISA resource strings) -------------------------
# THE NODES DO NOT GUESS which instrument is which -- you name them here, once.
# A node with no address comes up, reports connected=false and logs why; it will
# not go hunting on the USB bus. That is deliberate: picking "the first USB
# instrument" is how the galvo node ended up opening the TEMPERATURE controller,
# failing with EBUSY, and knocking its readings out on every 15 s retry.
#
# Find the addresses (with the backend stopped -- it holds the instruments):
#   cd ros2_ws
#   docker compose down
#   python3 scripts/list_instruments.py
#
# It prints every instrument it can see, identified, plus the exact line to
# paste here. Do not quote the value; no trailing comment on the same line.

# Rigol DG1022Z arbitrary-waveform generator -- drives the galvo mirrors.
#   USB0::6833::1602::DG1ZA000000000::0::INSTR      (pyvisa-py, decimal ids)
#   USB0::0x1AB1::0x0642::DG1ZA000000000::INSTR     (NI-VISA, hex ids)
#GALVO_RESOURCE=USB0::0x1AB1::0x0642::DG1ZA000000000::INSTR

# Wavelength Electronics TC10 LAB -- sample temperature controller.
# Three possible forms; use whichever list_instruments.py reports:
#   USB0::6725::12545::000000000::0::INSTR    USB through VISA
#   /dev/usbtmc0                              USB through the kernel usbtmc driver
#   TCPIP::192.168.1.50::INSTR                Ethernet unit
#
# On USB the two transports are mutually exclusive and whichever opens the
# instrument first wins: pyvisa/libusb DETACHES the kernel driver when it
# claims the device (so /dev/usbtmc0 disappears), and while the kernel driver
# holds it, libusb cannot open it -- it HANGS rather than failing. Both work;
# just do not expect both to be available at the same time.
#
# An ETHERNET unit can never be discovered (pyvisa-py does not scan the LAN),
# so it must always be named here.
#TCLAB_RESOURCE=/dev/usbtmc0

# ---- Camera frame size (optional) -----------------------------------------
# Passed to the camera service; defaults 640x480.
#CAM_W=640
#CAM_H=480
